{"context":"eka-steward 2.0.0 ships the orchestrator core (control loop, planner, pool, guard, approval, secrets broker, single EKA write path, observability) but exposes no consumable surface: the binary has only `version`, the engine lives in `internal/*` (not importable by any other module), and the human CLI cannot be driven programmatically. The steward repository's own ADR-0001 (`docs/adr/0001-standalone-binary.md`) rejected a `eka steward` shim as an eka-cli plugin, reasoning that the plugin host is deadline-bounded and runs a minimal environment, which it judged hostile to a long-running orchestrator. Reading the actual plugin model in eka-core/plugin and eka-cli/cmd shows it is a subprocess dispatcher: an official plugin is an executable named `eka-\u003cname\u003e` whose release assets follow `eka-\u003cname\u003e-\u003cos\u003e-\u003carch\u003e[.exe]` + SHA256SUMS.txt; its v1 manifest may carry an additive `commands` array, one CLI command per entry, dispatched as `eka-\u003cname\u003e \u003cfixed args\u003e \u003cuser args\u003e` with the CLI's stdin/stdout/stderr inherited, the exit code propagated, and DisableFlagParsing (the plugin owns its flags). Dispatch has NO CLI-added deadline (ctx is the root command's context; only the manifest/install CONTRACT probes are bounded by a timeout and a 1 MiB stdout cap). Dispatch runs under a bounded environment whitelist (PATH, HOME, EKA_PLUGIN_DIR, SystemRoot) gated official-registry-only plus a per-dispatch SHA-256 recompute (anti-TOCTOU, G1-G3). This corrects the 'deadline-bounded' half of the earlier reasoning recorded in adr:steward-acp-bridge and confirms the minimal-environment half. Two facts about steward make the environment the real constraint. (1) Credential delivery: steward's secrets broker resolves the tool-declared credential names (internal/driver: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENCODE_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY) from an injected Resolver and never reads os.Environ(); a file-backed resolver already exists (internal/secrets/file.go, NewFileResolver) but is not yet wired to a production caller, so under the stripped whitelist steward has no working credential source, and its workers run with a deliberately credential-free base (no HOME, no EKA_HOME, no PATH) that receives only the tool-declared names. (2) The store path: steward resolves its EKA store and checkpoint from EKA_HOME (falling back to HOME/.eka), and the whitelist does not include EKA_HOME. The maintainer's first consumer is eka-cli itself, before Atrium: steward's commands should be usable as `eka steward ...` without adding the binary to PATH. Important honesty note: the environment whitelist prevents secret INHERITANCE via the environment; it does NOT prevent filesystem access. HOME is whitelisted for every plugin, so any same-uid process can read a credential file under HOME. The effective trust boundary is the OS user, not the plugin.","decision":"Ship eka-steward as an OFFICIAL eka-cli plugin, exactly like eka-mcp: `eka plugin install steward` fetches and checksum-verifies the `eka-steward-\u003cos\u003e-\u003carch\u003e[.exe]` asset into the plugin directory and `eka steward \u003cargs\u003e` dispatches to `eka-steward \u003cargs\u003e`, so no system PATH entry is required and eka-cli is the single human entry point before Atrium. This amends repository ADR-0001 AND the 'deadline-bounded' rationale recorded in the accepted adr:steward-acp-bridge: steward stays a standalone binary with no in-process shim; the plugin is a distribution/dispatch adapter over the same binary. The environment gap is closed with option A now, plus a deferred follow-up filed as a work item (sto:steward-credential-keyring). Concretely: (a) wire the existing internal/secrets.NewFileResolver to the broker as steward's production credential source and harden it — open with O_NOFOLLOW, require a regular file owned by the current uid, require mode 0600 with no group/other permission bits, cap the file size, and refuse every violation deterministically; (b) add EKA_HOME to the plugin environment whitelist in eka-core/plugin and eka-cli/cmd (an ecosystem coordinate, not a secret); (c) state the trust boundary honestly — the whitelist prevents credential INHERITANCE via the environment, not filesystem access, so the effective boundary is the OS user and a same-uid process can read a user-owned file regardless of its mode. Option B/C (an OS keyring, or a per-connection broker over a unix socket / an inherited file descriptor with peer authentication) removes the secret from a same-uid-readable path and is the deferred hardening. The versioned machine protocol (JSON-RPC over stdio) remains the programmatic surface a host such as Atrium attaches to; the plugin is the human-facing path. Because dispatch inherits stdio and imposes no deadline, both the one-shot `run` and the long-running `serve` dispatch correctly; steward implements the plugin contract (`manifest --json` declaring contract v1 with a `commands` entry for `steward`, and a no-op/artifact `install`).","consequences":"The human path becomes `eka plugin install steward` then `eka steward \u003cargs\u003e` — no PATH install, discoverable, checksum-verified, exit-code faithful. eka-cli and eka-core gain a second official-registry entry and one environment coordinate (EKA_HOME); the plugin dispatch and trust model are unchanged. steward gains a plugin-contract surface (manifest, install) that must stay versioned and additive, plus a hardened file-backed credential source with a documented trust boundary. The whitelist change stays minimal (a non-secret path); no credential flows to any plugin through the environment. However, the file-backed secret IS readable by any same-uid process because HOME is whitelisted for all plugins, so option A reduces but does not eliminate the exposure; until option B/C ships, that exposure is documented explicitly and the trust boundary is stated as the OS user. Two surfaces (human plugin CLI + machine protocol) sit over one engine and must stay coherent. Cost: the plugin contract implementation and tests, the registry entry + EKA_HOME in eka-core/eka-cli, a hardened credential source with tests, and a tracked follow-up for secret delivery.","alternativesConsidered":"PATH install via install.sh (rejected: the maintainer wants no system PATH entry; the plugin directory is the managed, verified location). Go public library embed (rejected for now: only Go consumers benefit, the eventual Atrium stack may be non-Go, and promoting internal/* widens the API surface; the Resolver seam already provides the in-process extension point). MCP as the steward driver (rejected in repository ADR-0001 and adr:steward-machine-protocol: a request/response tool host is the wrong shape for a long-running supervising loop). Extending the plugin environment whitelist with credential variables for official plugins (rejected: it exposes the user's secrets to every official plugin process and grows the security contract; steward owns its credentials via the file resolver instead). Hardcoding a steward-specific full-environment dispatch in eka-cli (rejected: an ad-hoc special case that bypasses the plugin contract and its checksum gate). Secret delivery option B/C — an OS keyring (libsecret/Secret Service, macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager) or a per-connection broker over a unix socket / inherited fd with peer authentication (DEFERRED, not rejected: stronger than a HOME file because nothing is readable by a same-uid process; filed as sto:steward-credential-keyring)."}